---
import { OFFICIAL_SOURCES, SITE } from '../../config/site';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const FAQ_ITEMS = [
  {
    question: 'Why does an ACCA Manual J calculation matter more than tonnage per square foot?',
    answer:
      'Insulated attics, window shading, air tightness, and local humidity alter real heating and cooling loads dramatically. Rule-of-thumb oversizing causes 6-to-8-minute short cycles, clammy indoor air, and early compressor wear.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does a 10-year manufacturer warranty pay for technician labor and refrigerant?',
    answer:
      'Usually no. Standard factory warranties cover only defective replacement parts when registered promptly; diagnostic labor, brazing, refrigerant, and crane fees remain out-of-pocket unless backed by a written contractor labor warranty.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How should I check 2026 HVAC rebates claimed by a contractor?',
    answer:
      'Do not rely on expired federal IRS Section 25C credits for 2026 installations. Verify local electric utility or state rebates directly through DSIRE using the quoted AHRI Certified Reference Number.',
  },
] as const;

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.answer,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Vet an HVAC Contractor: Manual J & Licenses | HVAC Second Opinion"
  description="Vetting checklist for US homeowners: verify state licensing, EPA 608 certification, ACCA Manual J load calculations, written itemized bids, and labor warranties."
  structuredData={faqSchema}
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Calculator</a> / <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / <span>How to Vet an HVAC Contractor</span>
  </nav>

  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How to Vet an HVAC Contractor Before You Sign</h1>
    <p class="guide-meta">
      Last updated: <time datetime={SITE.lastUpdated}>November 24, 2026</time> · Checked against primary federal sources
    </p>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      Installation quality determines system lifespan just as much as the brand badge outside. A top-tier unit bolted onto leaking ducts without an ACCA load calculation or nitrogen purge will fail early—while a code-minimum <a href="/guides/seer2-explained/">SEER2 system</a> installed by a thorough crew runs cleanly for 15+ years.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Verify State License, Insurance, and EPA 608</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Active mechanical license:</strong> Check your state or city licensing board lookup portal for active standing and unresolved complaints.</li>
      <li><strong>Liability &amp; workers’ compensation:</strong> Request a certificate of insurance directly rather than accepting verbal assurances.</li>
      <li><strong>EPA Section 608 Certification:</strong> Required by <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaR22} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA regulations</a> for any technician handling R-22, R-410A, R-454B, or R-32 refrigerant.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Demand an ACCA Manual J Load Calculation</h2>
    <p>
      If a salesperson glances around your living room for three minutes and quotes a 4-ton unit using a “500 sq ft per ton” shortcut, pause immediately. Proper equipment sizing requires a room-by-room <strong>ACCA Manual J</strong> load calculation measuring roof and wall insulation R-values, window orientation and solar heat gain, air infiltration rates, local summer/winter design temperatures, and whether ducts run through unconditioned attics.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Why oversizing hurts comfort and lifespan:</strong> Installing a system half a ton or one ton too large blasts cold air for 6 to 8 minutes and shuts off (“short-cycling”). Because air conditioners need steady run cycles to wring moisture out of the indoor evaporator coil, oversized equipment leaves homes clammy while stressing compressor start windings.</li>
      <li><strong>Pair Manual J with Manual S and Manual D:</strong> Manual S selects exact equipment capacity at your local design temperature, while Manual D and a return static-pressure test confirm existing sheet-metal or flex ducts can move the required airflow without choking blower motors.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="manual-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/fig-06-manual-j-duct-static.svg"
        alt="Comparison of Bid 1 ($8,400 oversized 4-ton guess), Bid 2 ($9,900 ACCA Manual J 3-ton match), and Bid 3 ($13,600 variable upsell)."
        width="820"
        height="330"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Figure 06: Evaluating three competing replacement quotes—why Bid 2 ($9,900 Manual J match) beats both an oversized cheap bid and an overbuilt upsell.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>3. Require an Itemized Bid With AHRI Numbers</h2>
    <p>
      Insist on exact indoor coil and outdoor condenser model numbers plus an <strong>AHRI Certified Reference Number</strong> confirming compliance with <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.doeSeer2} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOE 10 CFR 430.32</a> regional standards. Review <a href="/guides/how-to-read-hvac-quote/">our line-by-line quote audit guide</a> to catch omitted permits, line sets, and plenum transitions.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Separate Equipment Parts Warranty From Labor Warranty</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Confirm in writing how long the <strong>contractor labor warranty</strong> runs, including what happens if a brazed joint leaks or a new drain clogs during the first cooling or heating season.</li>
      <li>Ask who submits the manufacturer limited parts warranty registration by the brand’s startup deadline (unregistered units often receive shorter default coverage terms), and ask for the confirmation receipt PDF for your house records.</li>
      <li>For service calls (<a href="/guides/signs-your-hvac-is-dying/">comparing repair vs. dying signs</a>), confirm upfront whether diagnostic fees credit toward repair and whether a 6-to-12-month workmanship guarantee covers follow-up visits if the part fails again.</li>
      <li>Pair warranty terms with Checks A through D in <a href="/guides/5000-rule/">our threshold guide</a> whenever repair costs climb past a few hundred dollars.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Verify 2026 Rebates on DSIRE Without Expired 25C Assumptions</h2>
    <p>
      Because <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.irs25c} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Section 25C federal credits</a> expired after December 31, 2025, verify any utility or state rebates through <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.dsire} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DSIRE</a> and confirm in writing whether <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.energyStar} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ENERGY STAR (15.2 SEER2 / 7.8 HSPF2)</a> AHRI thresholds apply.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked Example: Comparing Three Bids Using This Checklist</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        A homeowner replacing a failing 3.5-ton AC gathers three replacement bids after adding attic insulation:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bid 1 ($8,400):</strong> Skips Manual J and quotes a 4-ton unit “for hot August afternoons,” excludes municipal permit fees, and includes a 1-year labor warranty.</li>
        <li><strong>Bid 2 ($9,900):</strong> Completes a Manual J showing true peak cooling load of 31,800 Btu/h (2.65 tons), measures return duct static pressure, matches a <strong>3-ton</strong> 15.2 SEER2 system with AHRI certificate, includes municipal permits, EPA 608 recovery, new line set, and a 2-year labor warranty.</li>
        <li><strong>Bid 3 ($13,600):</strong> Runs Manual J matching 3 tons, pitches an 18.5 SEER2 variable-speed unit by counting an expired $2,000 federal 25C tax credit and claiming only ~$80/yr extra utility savings over Bid 2 ($3,700 premium = 46-year payback).</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <strong>Why Bid 2 wins cleanly:</strong> Bid 1 oversizes 3-ton ducts, triggering noisy airflow, short-cycling, and high humidity; Bid 3 relies on an expired tax credit for minimal extra bill savings. Bid 2 delivers verified Manual J sizing and full code-compliant scope at $9,900.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Three Questions to Take to Every Appointment</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>“Is this quote fully itemized—including permits, EPA 608 recovery, thermostat cable, plenum transitions, and a new line set?”</strong></li>
      <li><strong>“Did you perform a room-by-room ACCA Manual J load calculation and static-pressure check, and what AHRI number proves the system match?”</strong></li>
      <li><strong>“What warranty covers technician labor on this repair or install—if the same issue returns within 6 to 12 months, what is my out-of-pocket cost?”</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Run your competing repair and replacement figures through the <a href="/">HVAC Repair or Replace Calculator</a> to generate tailored questions automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{item.question}</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
